1
00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:03,850
George Washington. Wooden teeth.

2
00:00:04,000 --> 00:00:06,117
Nope. No wood at all.

3
00:00:06,267 --> 00:00:08,417
Washington: the wooden teeth myth.

4
00:00:08,567 --> 00:00:10,883
His teeth were terrible.

5
00:00:11,033 --> 00:00:14,883
By 1789, he had one tooth left.

6
00:00:15,033 --> 00:00:17,117
So he wore dentures.

7
00:00:17,267 --> 00:00:19,550
Made of hippo ivory.

8
00:00:19,700 --> 00:00:22,117
Cow teeth. Horse teeth.

9
00:00:22,267 --> 00:00:24,817
Metal springs and lead.

10
00:00:24,967 --> 00:00:27,017
And human teeth.

11
00:00:27,167 --> 00:00:31,150
Some were bought from enslaved people at Mount Vernon.

12
00:00:31,300 --> 00:00:34,150
The springs pushed his jaws apart.

13
00:00:34,300 --> 00:00:36,950
He had to clamp his mouth shut.

14
00:00:37,100 --> 00:00:40,917
That's the tight-lipped face on the one dollar bill.

15
00:00:41,067 --> 00:00:43,517
So where did the wood come from?

16
00:00:43,667 --> 00:00:46,950
Ivory stains and cracks over time.

17
00:00:47,100 --> 00:00:50,483
Old dentures can look like wood grain.

18
00:00:50,633 --> 00:00:53,650
Also, wood would rot in your mouth.

19
00:00:53,800 --> 00:00:56,417
Teeth made of wood: zero.

20
00:00:56,567 --> 00:01:00,750
Anyway, here's Washington with his wooden teeth.
